course: everyday
lesson: 288
title: Being gentle with small animals
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To a small animal like a hamster, a bird or a kitten, a person is a giant. Loud voices and quick grabbing hands look like danger to it.
explain: Animals feel safe with calm people. Moving slowly and speaking softly tells an animal that you are not a threat.
explain: Example: a girl in Auckland wants to hold her friend's guinea pig. She sits on the floor, waits quietly, lets it sniff her hand and lifts it gently with two hands, one under its bottom, while her friend watches. The guinea pig stays calm.
explain: A scared animal may scratch, bite, wriggle free and fall. Being gentle keeps both you and the animal safe.
fact: Always move slowly and speak softly around small animals.
fact: Animals feel safe with calm people.
fact: Quick movements and loud noises frighten small animals.
fact: A frightened animal may scratch, bite or try to escape.
fact: Let an animal sniff your hand before you touch it.
fact: Hold a small animal with both hands, supporting its body from underneath.
fact: Sit or kneel close to the floor when holding a small pet, so it cannot fall far.
fact: Never squeeze, chase or pick up a small animal by its tail or ears.
fact: Ask the owner before you touch someone else's pet.
fact: Leave a small animal alone when it hides or is sleeping.
exercise: How should you behave near a pet hamster? | Move slowly and speak softly | Run around it to make it play | Clap your hands to get its attention | Shout its name until it comes
exercise: Why do animals feel safe with calm people? | Calm people do not seem dangerous to them | Calm people always smell of food and treats | Animals cannot see calm people | Animals only like quiet people's clothes
exercise: Your friend's rabbit hops away when you run towards it. Why? | Quick movements look like danger to small animals | Rabbits only like to play chase, so it wants a game | The rabbit is being rude to you | Rabbits cannot see people who are running
exercise: What may a frightened kitten do if you grab it quickly? | Scratch or bite you and try to escape | Fall asleep at once in your arms | Purr because it likes being grabbed | Stay still because kittens never get scared
exercise: What should you do before stroking a dog or cat you have just met? | Ask the owner and let it sniff your hand | Pat it on the head straight away so it knows you | Hug it tightly to show you are friendly | Pull its tail gently to say hello
exercise: How should you hold a guinea pig? | With both hands, one supporting it underneath | By the scruff of its neck | By its ears so it cannot wriggle | With one hand around its tummy, squeezing it firmly
exercise: Why is it best to sit on the floor when holding a small pet? | If it wriggles free, it will not fall far | Pets only like people who sit down on the floor with them | Sitting makes the pet heavier to hold | It keeps the pet from seeing you
exercise: A budgie is sitting in its cage. How should you talk near it? | Softly and calmly | As loudly as you can | By whistling sharply in its ear | By banging on the cage first
exercise: Your little cousin wants to pick up the cat by its tail. What do you tell him? | Never lift an animal by its tail; it hurts and frightens it | It is fine if he does it quickly and puts the cat down soon | Cats like being lifted by the tail | Only lift it by the ears instead
exercise: The hamster has gone into its little house to sleep. What should you do? | Leave it to rest and play with it later | Shake the house to wake it up | Pull it out, because it's playtime and it's had enough sleep | Tap on the cage until it comes out
exercise: A boy chases a pigeon in the park to catch it. Is this kind? | No, chasing frightens the bird | Yes, birds enjoy being chased | Yes, it helps the bird exercise | No, but only because pigeons are dirty
exercise: Which is the gentlest way to make friends with a shy cat? | Sit quietly and let it come to you | Pick it up and carry it around | Follow it under the bed and keep trying to grab it | Call it loudly over and over
test: A girl in Dublin visits a farm with baby chicks. What is the right way to act? | Move slowly, speak quietly and listen to the farmer | Run among the chicks to see them all scatter and cheep loudly | Squeeze a chick to feel how soft it is | Shout so the chicks know she is there
test: Which is true? | A small animal sees a person as a giant, so quick moves scare it | Small animals are never afraid of people, even very big ones | Loud voices make pets feel safe | Animals like being chased by children
test: Your friend's mouse is on your hand and starts to wriggle. What should you do? | Lower your hands close to the floor and stay calm | Squeeze it so it stays still | Lift it higher in the air so it cannot jump off your hand | Shake your hand to calm it down
test: Why might a gentle child be scratched less often than a rough child? | Animals stay calm when they are handled gently | Gentle children have thicker skin, so the claws don't go in | Animals only scratch adults | Rough children are not allowed to hold pets
test: A boy wants to stroke a stranger's puppy in the street. What should he do first? | Ask the owner if he may | Grab the puppy before it runs off | Shout to make the puppy look at him | Pick it up to see if it is friendly
test: A girl holds a rabbit by its ears because "that is how farmers do it". Is she right? | No, holding a rabbit by its ears hurts it | Yes, ears are the safest place to hold it, they're like handles | Yes, but only for big rabbits | No, she should hold it by its tail
test: Your cat is hiding under the bed after a thunderstorm. What is kind? | Let it hide and come out when it feels safe | Pull it out by its legs | Poke it with a broom to make it come out from under there | Shout so it knows the storm is over
test: Why is speaking softly helpful around a small bird? | Calm sounds tell the bird you are not a threat | Birds cannot hear loud voices | Soft words make birds sing at once, and they love singing back | Loud voices are only rude to people
test: hamster hiding in his house, wanna cuddle him | Leave him be, let him come out when ready | Shake the little house till he comes out to play | Pull him out gently by the tail | Tap the cage to wake him up
test: how do i hold the guinea pig, by the ears? | No, two hands, support its body from under | Yes, ears are the safest grip | Hold it by the back legs | Hold it tight around the middle so it can't wriggle
test: neighbours bunny is so cute, can i just pick it up | Ask the owner first, it's their pet | Yes, all bunnies like being held | Yes, if you're quick about it | Only if the owner isn't looking
test: why sit on the floor holding the kitten | So it can't fall far if it wriggles | Kittens like being near the ground, it's warmer | It's easier to stroke it there | So it can't see you
explain: A girl in Canada picked up her hamster by squeezing it tight; it panicked, bit her finger and fell to the floor, hurting its leg.
explain: A boy in Ireland lifted a guinea pig while standing up; it wriggled free, fell, and broke its back leg, and the vet bill was huge.
fact: Small animals can be badly hurt or killed by a fall from the height of a standing child.
fact: A frightened small animal bites or scratches to protect itself, and those bites can become infected, so you wash a bite or scratch, cover it and tell an adult.
test: hamster keeps wriggling so im holding it tighter | Loosen up and sit on the floor, squeezing can hurt it | Hold it tighter and tighter so it can't escape your hands | Hold it by the tail instead | Shake it gently to calm it
test: cousin picked up the rabbit by its ears | Stop him; it hurts the rabbit, lift with both hands under it | It's fine, that's how farmers do it on the farm | It's fine for big rabbits | It's fine if it doesn't squeal
test: got scratched by the guinea pig, bit of blood | Wash it, cover it and tell an adult | Ignore it, it's tiny | Lick it clean | Hide it so the pet isn't blamed
test: did u hear a kid picked up a hamster standing up and it jumped and "died on the spot". lesson? | Sit low and hold small animals with both hands | Hold hamsters by the tail so they can't jump off your hand | Only pick them up when they're asleep | Hold them high so they feel safe
test: my brother says a guinea pig bit a girl's finger and "it got infected and her hand swelled up massive". what's the true bit | A scared small animal may bite and bites can get infected | Guinea pigs bite for no reason at all | Every animal bite makes your hand swell up massive like that | Small animal bites are always harmless
test: kids say the boy who got scratched by the class rabbit "was torturing it". nobody saw. spread it? | No, it's a rumour nobody saw; just handle animals gently | Yes, he must have been mean | Yes, and tell the teacher it's true so he gets told off properly | Yes, so he's banned from the rabbit
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct manner of holding a small animal? | With both hands, supporting the body from underneath | By the tail, so that it cannot struggle or escape from the hand | By the ears, with one hand only | Held high above the head
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a small pet should be held while sitting or kneeling near the floor. | A fall from a standing child's height may badly hurt or kill it | Small animals dislike tall people | It keeps the animal warmer | It prevents the animal from sleeping during the day
exam: It is given that a small animal is hiding or asleep. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | It shall be left alone | It shall be gently pulled out to play | It shall be woken with a loud noise | It shall be chased to exercise it
exam: State the conduct expected before touching a pet belonging to another person. | The owner shall be asked first | The pet may be touched provided it is small | The pet shall be picked up quickly before it escapes | The owner need be asked only for large dogs
